    "**“China had no 2008- or 1990-style crisis, so ABCT does not apply to China.”** — The first three boxes (injection, sector, trigger) have the same structure in China as in the US and Japan; what differs is the shape of the fourth: state banks, capital controls and administered prices split, share and shift the liquidation instead of letting it erupt in one place. “No acute crisis” and “no malinvestment” are different claims; Japan's thirty years are the precedent for the chronic form.",
    "**“The 4 trillion was fiscal stimulus; it had nothing to do with credit expansion.”** — The central government funded only about 1.2 trillion directly; the rest came from local matching and bank credit; new loans in 2009 were about 9.6 trillion and M2 grew about 28%. Bai, Hsieh and Song (2016) showed that the great bulk of the money landed as loans through financing vehicles. It was a credit program from the start.",
    "**“The three red lines deliberately burst the bubble, so the crisis was caused by policy.”** — The three red lines occupy the “trigger” box, the same slot as Japan's 1990 rate hikes and lending caps and America's 2004–06 hikes: they ended the expansion, but they did not create the malinvestment. That was piled up during the 2009–2020 credit expansion; the trigger only made it visible. Blaming the disease on the start of the treatment is the classic confusion from Stage 5.3.",
    "**“Industrial policy succeeded in EVs and solar, which proves the government picks tracks better than the market.”** — The success is real and must be conceded; but its cost lies off the chosen track: repressed deposit rates, subsidies funded by land finance, overcapacity and price wars from dozens of local governments recruiting simultaneously (polysilicon prices down about 90%, industry-wide losses), and the industries that never grew because they were not chosen. Competition inside the track is a discovery procedure; the choice of the track is not — the machinery's hit rate is above Soviet planning and below an open market.",
    "**“The local-debt swaps are liquidation; the problem is solved.”** — A swap replaces the vehicles' short, expensive debt with provinces' long, cheap debt: interest falls, maturities lengthen, risk moves from vehicle to budget. No project is thereby declared a failure and stopped; no asset is repriced and changes hands. It is malinvestment acknowledged and repriced but still held by the public sector — between liquidation and Japanese evergreening. Whether it evolves into a slow liquidation depends on whether “no new hidden debt” is enforced, which is observable.",
